#import "../../lib/24xx.typ": *

#show: xx.with(
  title: "AX&X: The Retinue",
  subtitle: "Hirelings, henchmen, and the hands you lead. A followers supplement for AX&X.",
  cols: 2,
)

A party is rarely only its player characters. You pay hands to carry the light and the loot, and you win loyal companions who level at your side. That is your retinue: the #k[hirelings] you hire by the day and the #k[henchmen] you earn by the deed. None of it needs a funnel. A man-at-arms signs on in any town with coin on the table. The core prices the common hands, and the zero-level person they are built from lives in the Funnel supplement; this book is how you hire them, use them, and keep them.

= Hirelings

A hireling is a hand you pay by the day, no loyalty asked beyond the wage and fair treatment.

- #item[The Hired][Torchbearer or porter #k[1 GP/day], a zero level, built as the Funnel supplement sets out. Link-boy, bearer, and camp-hand are the same. A #k[man-at-arms] at #k[5 GP/day] is Level 1, the core's hireling: HD 1, HP 6, AC 13, Atk +1, 1d6.]
- #item[Shares][A hireling draws its wage and a #k[half share] of XP. Let that XP ride and a hireling can reach the Level 1 threshold and graduate just as a funnel survivor does, choosing a class and coming up a henchman.]
- #item[Loyalty][Asked first to risk death, a hireling makes a #k[CHA check], yours, or balks, flees, or quits. After that it tests #k[morale] like any creature: 1d6, breaking on 1 to 3 when the blood runs. Fair pay, fair shares, and not being spent as bait all weigh for you, and the GM may shade the roll.]
- #item[Specialists][A trade expert, an alchemist, a sage, a sapper, costs more and bargains harder, but brings a knack no common hireling has. Price it to the job.]

= Using a Retinue

Why a party hires at all, and how the old tables spent their hired hands. A retinue is not color, it is the margin that keeps the heroes alive and the treasure moving.

- #item[Carry the Light][A hand on a torch is a hand off a shield, and swinging at what you cannot see is #k[-4]. A hired #k[torchbearer] frees a fighter's hands and walks in the protected middle of the march, the light thrown forward over the front rank.]
- #item[Carry the Loot][Gold is XP, but only once hauled home, and coin fills slots. A #k[porter] or bearer multiplies what you carry out in one trip, so the back pays its wage in experience. Bearers and mules wait at the rim of a tight crawl, a shaft, or deep water; they cannot follow everywhere.]
- #item[Thicken the Line][#k[Men-at-arms] hold the front and the flanks and screen the spellcaster from the first rush. Sent ahead to prod a floor or try a door, they are the party's margin against the trap, the grim bargain of the hired hand.]
- #item[Not Fodder][Spend them as pure bait and word spreads: the next town's hands want no part of you, and the ones you have break and walk. Fair pay, a fair share, and reasonable risk are what keep a retinue, and a loyal hand outlives a used one.]

= Henchmen

A henchman is not hired but won: a loyal adventurer who throws in with you for a share and a cause, levels at your side, and may one day carry the torch when you fall.

- #item[How Many][You may lead henchmen up to your #k[CHA modifier + 1], minimum one. A charmer draws a following; a boor leads no one.]
- #item[Where They Come From][A funnel survivor you kept, a captive you freed, a Lifepath Comrade or Retainer, a faction's gift, or a likely sort won in a tavern with a #k[CHA check]. Each is a full character, rolled up, entering at Level 1 or a Level below you.]
- #item[The Deal][No wage. A henchman takes a #k[half share] of loot and XP and #k[levels on it], and gets a say in how both are split. A henchman is owed that share by right.]
- #item[Loyalty][Loyalty is won by use. Share fairly, spend them as comrades not fodder, and they hold. Cheat or risk them past reason and loyalty frays: a CHA check or a morale test when pushed, and a broken henchman walks, or turns, a ready-made Rival or Enemy.]
- #item[The Succession][When your character dies, a henchman is the replacement the core already promises: leveled, equipped, and tied to the party. Promote one, and roll a fresh henchman in their place.]

= From Peasant to Hero

One ladder runs under all of it. A zero level is the bottom rung, peasant, torchbearer, and porter alike. Earn a half share through a funnel or a hired season, cross the Level 1 threshold, and pick a class to graduate. Throw in for a cause and a share, and a graduate becomes a henchman who levels with you. Outlive your patron, or strike out alone, and a henchman becomes a hero in their own right. When the whole crowd takes the field, the Warband supplement runs them as one host, the hired and the won mustering under a banner.
